#!/usr/bin/env sh

# Script: session-lock-stranded
# Purpose: Report whether the compositor holds an ext-session-lock that NO LIVE
#          LOCKER owns
# Requirements: Arch Linux, hyprctl, jaq, flock (util-linux)
# Usage: session-lock-stranded  (no output; the answer is the exit status)
#
# Exit: 0 stranded · 1 not stranded · 2 undetermined
#
# 🚨 `session-locked` alone is NOT this question, and treating it as if it were
# displaces a live lock screen. It answers "does the compositor hold a lock",
# which is true both when a locker is running and when one died leaving the
# failsafe up -- and misc:allow_session_lock_restore means a second client is
# now ACCEPTED rather than refused, so acting on the first answer takes the
# screen out from under whoever is typing into the running locker. Measured
# 2026-09-12: a shell restart while hypridle's hyprlock was up did exactly that.
#
# The second condition is the lock file `immediate-lock` already uses. Its
# descriptor is inherited by the exec'd locker and released only when that
# process exits, so a HELD lock file means a live locker and a FREE one means
# nothing is holding the compositor's lock open.
#
# ⚠️ It answers about lockers OTHER than the caller. The Quickshell lock surface
# takes no lock file -- it is in-process, and its own state is a better guard
# than a file -- so a caller that can itself be the locker must rule that out
# first, the way lock/LockScreen.qml does before it ever runs this.
#
# ⚠️ 2 is a real third answer, inherited from session-locked: Hyprland stops at
# the first blocking reason, so a monitor with no workspace yet reports
# WORKSPACE and the lock is never reached. A retrying caller must not accept it.

DIR=$(dirname -- "$0")

"$DIR/session-locked"
state=$?

case "$state" in
1) exit 1 ;;
0) ;;
*) exit 2 ;;
esac

LOCKFILE="${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/run/user/$(id -u)}/immediate-lock.lock"

exec 9>"$LOCKFILE" || exit 2
flock -n 9 || exit 1

exit 0
